Why the container:
How do containers come to the rescue?

Containers package an application together with all of its dependencies, libraries, runtime, and configuration into a single unit. Because of this, the application runs the same way regardless of the environment (developer laptop, test server, or production).

Benefits of containers:

Simplified setup: Applications can be deployed quickly without manually installing dependencies.
Portability: The same container image can run on any system that supports a container runtime (such as Docker).
Consistent environments: Eliminates the "it works on my machine" problem by ensuring identical environments across development, testing, and production.
Isolation: Each container runs independently, preventing conflicts between applications or their dependencies.
Efficiency: Containers share the host operating system kernel, making them lighter and faster than traditional virtual machines.
Better resource control: CPU, memory, and storage limits can be assigned to individual containers, improving resource utilization.
Easy scalability: Applications can be scaled horizontally by starting multiple container instances, making them well suited for cloud-native and microservices architectures.

Components of the Virtualization Stack
Hardware Infrastructure:
The physical server resources such as CPU, memory, storage, and network.
Host Operating System:
The operating system installed directly on the physical hardware.
Examples: Windows Server, Ubuntu, Red Hat Enterprise Linux.
Hypervisor:
Software that creates and manages Virtual Machines (VMs).
It allocates CPU, memory, storage, and networking to each VM.
Examples: VMware ESXi, Microsoft Hyper-V, KVM, Oracle VirtualBox.
Virtual Machines (VMs)
Each VM contains:
Guest OS: A complete operating system (Windows, Linux, etc.)
Dependencies (Deps): Libraries and packages required by the application.
Application Code: The software being executed.



| Feature             | Virtual Machines (VMs)                                                                            | Docker Containers                                                                             |
| ------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **Isolation**       | **Strong isolation** – Each VM has its own operating system, providing complete isolation.        | **Process-level isolation** – Containers share the host OS kernel.                            |
| **Size / Overhead** | **Larger** – Includes a full guest OS and virtual hardware, consuming more CPU, RAM, and storage. | **Lightweight** – Shares the host kernel, so it starts faster and uses fewer resources.       |
| **Portability**     | **Less portable** – May depend on the hypervisor and guest OS configuration.                      | **Highly portable** – Can run consistently across different environments that support Docker. |


Running a container from an image

Issue docker run in the CLI.
CLI sends a request to the host's REST API.
Docker Host checks if the image is present in the local cache.
If not, it downloads it from the image registry.
Docker Host instantiates a new container based on the image.


Building and Pushing an Image

Issue docker build in the CLI.
CLI sends a request to the host's REST API.
This also includes the respective Dockerfile and build context.
Docker Host builds the image according to the Dockerfile.
Docker Host tags the image and stores it locally.
Issue the docker push command in the CLI.
